From ae256ee11b9bdc529fa30de66b5a38d1218f295b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stephan-xyz <stephan@zehrer.xyz>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 16:01:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dwc2d: mice poll like keyboards; small helper thread stacks
 (#798)

- Keyboards and mice share the polling of #787 (InterruptPoll in
  hid.rs): no busy wait without a transaction translator, every 30 ms
  while idle behind a high-speed hub. A low-speed mouse behind the
  Zero 2 W hub cost about 8 % of a core. The data is read out of the DMA
  buffer before the next packet is started into it.
- After 10 failed polls in a row a keyboard or mouse is polled every
  500 ms until it works again or the rescan removes it, with a log line
  each way: behind a hub each failed poll waited its whole 5 ms timeout.
- The input and test-file threads get 64 KiB stacks instead of 2 MiB;
  each keyboard or mouse added 2 MB to dwc2d.
- While /tmp/dwc2d-input-log exists, key events are printed too, like
  mouse events (tests without a person).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
